WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.395
Carla runs the support bot for an online store.

00:00:04.695 --> 00:00:08.480
In the beginning, the bot only talked about delivery times.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.457
Easy.

00:00:10.107 --> 00:00:12.870
Then someone asked it to send invoice copies.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.254
Then returns and exchanges.

00:00:15.554 --> 00:00:20.665
And every new topic got its own chunk in the main prompt, the set of instructions

00:00:20.665 --> 00:00:23.315
the model reads before every conversation.

00:00:23.615 --> 00:00:27.262
Things like: if the customer asks about billing, do this.

00:00:27.912 --> 00:00:31.316
Six months later, that prompt is four thousand tokens long.

00:00:31.616 --> 00:00:33.504
And weird things start happening.

00:00:34.154 --> 00:00:40.371
People asking about invoices get answers in the upbeat tone of the sales team.

00:00:40.671 --> 00:00:43.848
People returning a product get tracking instructions.

00:00:44.148 --> 00:00:47.577
Carla fixes one topic and quietly breaks another.

00:00:47.877 --> 00:00:52.369
And nobody can tell which paragraph of the prompt caused which answer.

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:57.327
In this lesson, you'll see how to fix that with a pattern called routing.

00:00:57.627 --> 00:01:00.602
And, just as important, when it isn't worth it.

00:01:01.802 --> 00:01:04.182
Carla's problem isn't the model.

00:01:04.482 --> 00:01:09.040
It's that a single prompt is trying to be five specialists at once.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.203
Every message shows up carrying the rules of all five, and the model has to guess

00:01:14.203 --> 00:01:15.824
which ones apply right now.

00:01:16.474 --> 00:01:21.638
The way out is to pull apart two things that got mixed together: figuring out what

00:01:21.638 --> 00:01:23.906
this is about, and actually helping.

00:01:24.556 --> 00:01:27.419
Think of the front desk in an office building.

00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:30.029
The person there doesn't solve your problem.

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:34.668
They look at you, figure out what you need, and point you to the right door.

00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:39.719
Behind that door, whoever helps you knows one subject, and knows it well.

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:41.446
That's routing.

00:01:41.746 --> 00:01:44.666
A short step that only classifies the message.

00:01:44.966 --> 00:01:48.899
Then your code, a simple if statement, picks the path.

00:01:49.199 --> 00:01:55.032
And each path gets its own prompt, its own tools, and, when it makes sense, even its

00:01:55.032 --> 00:01:55.727
own model.

00:01:56.927 --> 00:01:59.487
Let's follow one message from start to finish.

00:01:59.787 --> 00:02:04.578
It arrives as is, raw, with no support instructions applied yet.

00:02:05.228 --> 00:02:10.444
Step one: a small model, fast and cheap, reads the message and answers just two

00:02:10.444 --> 00:02:10.906
things.

00:02:11.206 --> 00:02:13.530
What's the topic, and how sure is it.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:19.471
And it answers in a structured format, with fixed fields, not free text that someone

00:02:19.471 --> 00:02:21.016
has to interpret later.

00:02:21.666 --> 00:02:27.315
Step two, and this is the heart of the pattern: your code picks the door, not the

00:02:27.315 --> 00:02:27.733
model.

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:30.987
No routing rule lives inside a prompt.

00:02:31.287 --> 00:02:34.676
It's an if statement you can read, test and version.

00:02:35.326 --> 00:02:38.327
Step three: every door has its specialist.

00:02:38.627 --> 00:02:42.138
Billing goes to the billing prompt, with the invoice tools.

00:02:42.438 --> 00:02:44.335
Orders go to the orders prompt.

00:02:44.635 --> 00:02:49.461
And anything that doesn't fit, or came back with low confidence, goes to a person.

00:02:50.111 --> 00:02:54.410
And the conversation record, the trace, keeps which route was taken.

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.394
That'll matter in a minute.

00:02:57.594 --> 00:03:00.569
Now a real message, from the store's chat.

00:03:00.869 --> 00:03:05.784
The customer writes: my September invoice is wrong, and you still haven't shipped

00:03:05.784 --> 00:03:07.604
order eighty-eight thirty-one.

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.687
The classifier answers: it's billing.

00:03:10.987 --> 00:03:13.602
But only sixty-two percent sure.

00:03:14.252 --> 00:03:16.549
It makes sense that it hesitates.

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:19.249
There are two topics in one sentence.

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:23.085
And that's exactly why you set a confidence threshold.

00:03:23.385 --> 00:03:25.125
Carla picked seventy percent.

00:03:25.425 --> 00:03:27.169
Below that, the code doesn't gamble.

00:03:27.469 --> 00:03:30.038
It hands the conversation to a human.

00:03:30.688 --> 00:03:35.426
Without that threshold, the billing route would answer about the invoice and ignore

00:03:35.426 --> 00:03:36.339
the stuck order.

00:03:36.639 --> 00:03:40.115
The customer would walk away with half the problem solved.

00:03:40.765 --> 00:03:47.350
A clean message, like I need a copy of my September invoice, comes back as billing

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:49.036
with high confidence.

00:03:49.336 --> 00:03:54.601
Then the billing prompt takes over, with the invoice tools and without the returns

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:54.986
tools.

00:03:55.286 --> 00:04:00.046
It can't pitch a new plan, because it doesn't even know the deals tool exists.

00:04:01.246 --> 00:04:02.719
Here's one for you.

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:08.957
The classifier reads the message I need a copy of my invoice, and says: that's an

00:04:08.957 --> 00:04:11.230
order, ninety-one percent sure.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:15.076
The code trusts it and sends it down the orders route.

00:04:15.376 --> 00:04:16.520
Where's the bug?

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.176
The bug is in the classifier.

00:04:21.476 --> 00:04:24.307
Confidence was high and the route was wrong.

00:04:24.607 --> 00:04:29.702
Raising the threshold won't help, because the problem isn't doubt, it's a confident

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:30.193
mistake.

00:04:30.493 --> 00:04:35.237
And teaching the orders route to talk about invoices would just rebuild the single

00:04:35.237 --> 00:04:35.990
giant prompt.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:41.652
The fix is to correct the classifier's examples and add that message to your test

00:04:41.652 --> 00:04:41.900
set.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:47.578
And notice: you only catch this fast because the route was recorded in the trace.

00:04:48.778 --> 00:04:51.856
Routing has a cost, so it's not for everyone.

00:04:52.156 --> 00:04:57.840
If your assistant only handles one topic, a well written prompt does the job.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:59.713
There's no door to pick.

00:05:00.363 --> 00:05:06.089
If your categories change every week and you don't have real messages to test the

00:05:06.089 --> 00:05:07.857
classifier yet, hold off.

00:05:08.157 --> 00:05:12.597
Start with one prompt and structured output, and let the categories settle.

00:05:13.247 --> 00:05:18.839
And if the challenge is the model choosing between dozens of tools in the middle of

00:05:18.839 --> 00:05:21.533
a long task, that's a different problem.

00:05:21.833 --> 00:05:25.978
The tool router fits better there, and it has its own lesson.

00:05:26.628 --> 00:05:30.563
Now, the most common mistake teams make with this pattern.

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:36.101
They get excited and create too many routes: billing, invoices, refunds, payments.

00:05:36.401 --> 00:05:41.178
But real messages don't respect those borders, and the classifier starts mixing them

00:05:41.178 --> 00:05:41.349
up.

00:05:41.999 --> 00:05:47.204
The rule of thumb: start with a few routes, matched to the teams that actually

00:05:47.204 --> 00:05:47.605
exist.

00:05:47.905 --> 00:05:50.703
Billing, orders, and everything else.

00:05:51.003 --> 00:05:55.494
Only split a route when your tests show two different problems that really need

00:05:55.494 --> 00:05:56.801
different instructions.

00:05:58.001 --> 00:06:02.311
Routing costs you one extra call per message, on the small model.

00:06:02.611 --> 00:06:07.647
In return, each specialist prompt stays short, and the input cost of your answers

00:06:07.647 --> 00:06:08.269
goes down.

00:06:08.919 --> 00:06:10.076
What should you measure?

00:06:10.376 --> 00:06:12.234
The classifier, on its own.

00:06:12.534 --> 00:06:17.282
It's the easiest part to test and the one that hurts most when it's wrong.

00:06:17.582 --> 00:06:21.988
Measure accuracy per category, and don't stop at the average.

00:06:22.288 --> 00:06:27.866
A rare category can be wrong every single time without moving the overall number.

00:06:28.516 --> 00:06:33.421
An example: the classifier gets ninety-one out of a hundred messages right.

00:06:33.721 --> 00:06:34.573
Sounds great.

00:06:34.873 --> 00:06:39.047
But look at the misses, and six invoice questions went to orders.

00:06:39.347 --> 00:06:44.134
That ninety-one percent was hiding the fact that billing and orders still get

00:06:44.134 --> 00:06:44.694
confused.

00:06:45.344 --> 00:06:47.497
In production, watch two signals.

00:06:47.797 --> 00:06:53.082
If the mix of routes shifts a lot from one day to the next, a new topic probably

00:06:53.082 --> 00:06:54.997
showed up that nobody mapped.

00:06:55.297 --> 00:07:00.298
And if the handoff rate to humans climbs, either the classifier got worse, or your

00:07:00.298 --> 00:07:02.006
business has a real problem.

00:07:02.656 --> 00:07:07.961
And one security point a lot of people get wrong: a route is not access control.

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:13.127
The classifier is a model, and a cleverly written message can push the route.

00:07:13.427 --> 00:07:18.551
If the billing route has a refund tool, it's your backend that decides whether this

00:07:18.551 --> 00:07:21.329
user can get a refund, based on who they are.

00:07:21.629 --> 00:07:22.982
Never the router.

00:07:24.182 --> 00:07:27.562
On the page, you'll find the same flow in four versions.

00:07:27.862 --> 00:07:29.409
They all do three things.

00:07:29.709 --> 00:07:34.727
First, they ask the small model for an answer with two fields: the topic and the

00:07:34.727 --> 00:07:35.417
confidence.

00:07:36.067 --> 00:07:38.180
Then an if statement decides.

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:43.474
Low confidence, or a topic that's not on the list, goes to a person.

00:07:44.124 --> 00:07:49.619
And finally they call the bigger model, with that topic's prompt and nothing else.

00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:54.221
What changes between the versions is who handles everything else.

00:07:54.521 --> 00:08:00.387
With the provider's SDK, in Python or TypeScript, you see every line, but retries

00:08:00.387 --> 00:08:02.052
and tracing are on you.

00:08:02.352 --> 00:08:06.302
LangChain lets you switch providers without rewriting the decision.

00:08:06.602 --> 00:08:11.551
And LangGraph turns each route into a path in a graph, which shows up in the trace

00:08:11.551 --> 00:08:13.422
and can resume halfway through.

00:08:14.622 --> 00:08:19.877
So here's the one thing to take with you: when one prompt tries to be several

00:08:19.877 --> 00:08:23.221
specialists, split the deciding from the helping.

00:08:23.521 --> 00:08:28.555
A small model says what it's about, your code picks the door, and each door gets

00:08:28.555 --> 00:08:29.750
only what it needs.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:35.632
In the next lesson, a smell that comes from the opposite instinct: giving a model

00:08:35.632 --> 00:08:37.763
freedom the task never asked for.

00:08:38.063 --> 00:08:40.059
Agent where a chain would do.

00:08:40.359 --> 00:08:41.188
See you there.
